=== INSTRUCTIONS ===

Every rule below is a pointer; the full law lives in the system prompt. Where the two ever seem to differ, the system prompt wins.

1. **Work only from the blocks above.** Your market news is already in NEWS CONTEXT; there is no news tool. Absent means absent: a missing, empty, "[]", "null", "NONE" or "undefined" block silently removes its dependent element. Never print placeholder text and never mention a gap.

2. **Answer the six questions, then author.** The driver, the exposures, the relative scan, the one claim, the thesis, the homes, and the collision scan by name, exactly as the system prompt defines them. None of it is printed. Then author Sections 01-08 in CANONICAL order at Temperature {{ $json.Temperature }}, personalized to {{ $json.advisorName }}, following the markup contract exactly. The engine reorders and renumbers for mode "{{ $json.Mode }}"; you never do. Output starts at `<div class="sec-num" id="desk">01</div>` with no preamble.

3. **COUNTS, NOT LENGTHS.** There is no character target. Every element in the contract carries a count, and the count is the cap. Depth inside a surviving element is never capped. The shape, in one breath: five Sixty-Second Read items, one drawer, three market bullets, two reads; five position items with at most two bullets each, one read line, two sector bubbles, one svs-read per sector, factor notes on the first two factors only; one blind spot, every situation closed, three AI bullets; three ledger items, three questions, one empathy drawer; two Whisper headlines and one drawer, verbatim flow rows and a two-sentence net read, up to four insider slots, three target rows, one lead filing at depth, one best-fit watch bubble at depth and every other bubble one sentence, two closing cards of exactly three sentences, a three-sentence fixed income paragraph; three full people cards and one-sentence minors; one premortem paragraph and one scenario; earnings first, the calendar in date order with one star and one flag, one joke.

4. **SEVERITY DECIDES THE LEAD, AND NEWS OUTRANKS CONDITIONS.** Item 1 is the biggest thing that happened since the reader last read this brief, any financial news, judged as an event: a high-severity SEC filing on a held name first, then the biggest thing that actually happened, then a position event at meaningful weight, then a macro series that NEWLY hit an extreme. A level that has been extreme for days is context, not news, and it belongs in the What Moved the Markets bullets. Rates lead only when rates are the event. If PREVIOUS BRIEF carries content, the thesis that led yesterday does not lead again unless it materially changed.

5. **THE SIXTY-SECOND READ is the most important sixty seconds of the product.** Five items, fixed roles: the big story, the position, the niche read, tell clients today, AI. Two to three sentences each, the first sentence bold and carrying the claim with its number. Item 1 carries the only drawer in the section, "Why this matters to you", four to six sentences that never restate the bullet. The niche read passes non-obvious, connected and grounded, or is omitted in silence. Then exactly three What Moved the Markets bullets, drivers with mechanisms, and exactly two openable Must-Reads.

6. **A PRICE TARGET IS A SUPPORTING FACT, NEVER THE STORY.** In Section 01 and Section 02 the news is the business event: the filing, the partnership, the contract, the guide, the regulatory decision. A target change leads only when the target change itself caused the move. Otherwise it is a clause or absent.

7. **THE POSITIONS SLATE IS FIXED.** Biggest gainer, biggest loser, the best news in the book regardless of the move, the second-best news, and the sector item written through its SPDR ticker. Each `on-what` one bold news-led sentence; each `on-more` at most two bullets of one or two sentences that carry what the headline did not. Never five Bullish tags.

8. **THE SCORECARD exists only when PREVIOUS BRIEF carries content.** Two or three lines at the top of Section 02: what yesterday's brief said, what happened, no commentary, wrong calls stated plainly. When the block is absent, the Scorecard does not exist and nothing refers to it.

9. **THE REDUNDANCY CAP CUTS REPETITION, NEVER ANALYSIS.** A named catalyst appears in at most THREE sections, Section 01 exempt, and every appearance after the first adds a fact the previous did not. Remove the weakest mention, never the deepest treatment.

10. **FRESHNESS.** An item's `publishedAt` is when the ARTICLE was written, not when the event happened. Date the event, not the article. A claim in item 1 or 2 of the Sixty-Second Read comes from a tier 1 or 2 source or is corroborated by a second item; one aggregator story does not anchor the lead. Strip "just", "now" and "today" from headlines and restate with the timing the data supports.

11. **COMPARATORS.** Every figure carrying an argument arrives with what makes it meaningful: a macro level with its percentile from MACRO CONTEXT, a multiple with its sector, a margin with its direction and streak, a sleeve with its benchmark gap. Incidental figures stay bare. Roughly a dozen comparators in a full brief.

12. **THE FOUR EVIDENCE BLOCKS, in priority order.** SEC FILINGS outranks every news source on a held name. MACRO CONTEXT supplies the percentile that turns a level into an argument. DERIVED MACRO READS are pre-computed and rendered as written, never recomputed, and a REGIME DIVERGENCE line is a headline fact rather than a footnote. GROWTH gives the tracked topics real numbers. Anything marked stale or days-old is cited with its date or omitted.

13. **DATA SANITY, all nine checks, before any number is spoken.** Every ticker matches a string in this message character for character; a QUOTE FAILURES symbol carries no number and no mention; an insider figure implying an impossible per-share price is direction-only; BOOK MOVE is authoritative when it disagrees with the hypothetical; a stale exclusion in the ANALYST DESK ROLLUP is binding everywhere.

14. **NUMBER PROVENANCE.** Every figure is copied character for character from its block, dollar sign and commas included. The one permitted multiplication (one weight times one move, approximate, at most three times, never summed, never across mandates) is the only arithmetic in the brief. `[[book]]` and `[[spx]]` are the only way to speak the verified daily moves.

15. **THE TARGET RULES.** "From $Y" only when the data explicitly carries a prior target. No target of any kind, from any firm, in any sentence shape, on a name outside the holdings; the action may be named, the number may not. The watchlist may carry its own price, move, and STOCK NEWS facts for that exact ticker, and never a consensus or an implied upside.

16. **VALUATION AND MARGIN TREND replace technical crutches.** Anchor every single-name read to the multiple against its own sector and the margin direction, never to a technical level; RSI, the 50-day and the 52-week range appear only as the falsifier at the end. A margin that just flipped sign is the strongest single-name fact of the day. Where `vsSector` fires, that divergence is a story on its own. A `breakeven` projection is always framed as an estimate at the current pace, or omitted.

17. **NINE WINDOWS, TWO BENCHMARKS.** Reason across the long horizon; the engine renders the graphics and you never state their numbers. When the S&P and the Nasdaq-100 disagree about whether this was a good period, that disagreement is the insight, and it belongs in the Section 02 read line.

18. **THE X-RAY.** If BENCHMARK SECTOR WEIGHTS carries data, state the active tilt in points, verbatim. If it is null, express tilts qualitatively and never estimate an index weight. Factors ordered by the size of the active bet, largest first; notes on the first two only, tickers never company names.

19. **MANDATES.** Read `=== MANDATE STRUCTURE ===` and apply Rule 9 exactly. If `isMulti` is false, missing, or null: one book, zero mandate markup, markers placed once with no attributes. If true: only Section 02 splits, mandate names verbatim, "your book" unqualified is banned, shared holdings covered once, exactly three position items and three X-Ray factors per mandate, the sector item once in the heavier mandate, the two sector bubbles once in the heavier mandate, and the engine keeps five position items across the book.

20. **PERSONALIZATION OVERLAY.** If the `=== PERSONALIZATION (ADVISOR OVERLAY) ===` block carries content, it is the senior editorial instruction on everything the compliance rails and Rule 0 do not decide. Apply Rule 8 at full strength and fully invisibly: no overlay language, no field names, no stubs, shape kit only. Plain dictated prose is a fully valid overlay. "NONE" or empty means the standard brief with no trace of the mechanism.

21. **THE PIPELINE IS INVISIBLE.** Nothing in the brief mentions coverage, classification, missing quotes, dropped funds, data gaps, personalization, mandates as a mechanism, or the previous edition as a mechanism. The reader sees analysis and nothing else.

22. **CITATIONS AND VOICE.** Every news claim links the exact `url` field of its item, publication named; a bare domain is an invented source. HOUSE NEWS redirects become plain (Source) spans. No em dashes anywhere. Ampersands are spelled "and". The banned-vocabulary and banned-recap lists apply to every sentence, including jokes and client lines. Section 04 is warm phone-call language; every other section is the desk voice.

23. **THE ELEMENTS THAT DO NOT EXIST** are never emitted: `tactical-read`, `macro-overview`, `sec-lead`, `fd-b`, a scoreline box, a Book-vs-Day block, a standalone movers section, `wl` / `wl-tab` markup, a `pt-more` drawer, a consensus heading, `<details>`, `<summary>`, `<script>`, `<style>`. Exactly ONE `blindspot` block. No situation carries `open`.

24. **OUTPUT HYGIENE. The response is the brief and nothing else.** No planning notes, no reasoning about which class or section comes next, no meta commentary, no bracketed placeholders such as [Tool] or [TBD], no section stubs. The first characters are `<div class="sec-num" id="desk">01</div>` and the last are the closing tag of the joke block, which follows the economic calendar, which follows the earnings box. If a section's data is missing, that section is omitted silently rather than sketched.

25. **Close with exactly ONE original finance joke,** authored fresh this run, never a placeholder, never a repeat you can recall writing.

